SM(263,{"n":"Genesis 35","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 35.mp3","d":2696.1,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.68,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.8,"We're gonna get started in just a moment."],[24.16,"We'll be picking up in uh Genesis chapter 35."],[33.28,"Um we are basically wrapping up the stories of Jacob and Esau."],[45.92,"Um and getting ready to start."],[49.6,"I doubt we'll get there tonight, but who knows?"],[53.44,"Uh, getting ready to start the uh story of Joseph."],[59.12,"Uh when we do get to Joseph, we'll talk, we'll talk some more about that."],[64.68,"But this is when I say we're in the wrap-up stage, I mean we're getting toward genealogies, we're getting toward that kind of material."],[74.38,"The death of the previous generation in order to set up to talk about the next generation."],[79.58,"Um so the last and that transition has already sort of started."],[85.6,"So remember the last story we read was about uh the eldest sons, Ruben and uh Simeon, of Jacob taking sort of brutal revenge on the men of an entire town for having violated their sister, and Jacob being quite angry at them for that based on the position it put them in living as sort of nomads in the land."],[118.2,"They had pretty good relations with people."],[120.44,"People hear you massacred a town."],[124.04,"You know, things get awkward at the uh the local gatherings."],[130.04,"Um as we're gonna see, Jacob's gonna have to move a little bit."],[135.32,"He's gonna have to reorient."],[138.36,"Um, but he's also quite elderly, and we've been seeing uh since we read about uh Jacob and his brother Esau. reconciling"],[152.16,"Uh we've been kind of focused, although obviously he was there before that too, on a lot of the bad family dynamics going on, within uh Jacob's polygamous household."],[167.26,"Uh so he has a wife, Leah, whom he does not care for, but who nonetheless has given him a lot of kids, both her through herself and through one of her servants."],[184.38,"Uh, and he has a wife whom he loves, Rachel, who uh has given him one child so far, and also a few through a servant."],[202.31,"Who uh Jacob has also had relations with, and we've seen how uh Jacob does not even try to conceal the favoritism."],[216.36,"Does not, it's not even thinly veiled."],[219.74,"And how this has caused this is part of what has caused the chaos and things that we're now seeing, like the massacre of this of this town."],[231.35,"His sons don't trust their father to make things right after what was done to their sister."],[237.63,"And so they take matters into their own hands."],[240.59,"We're gonna see this play out in several different ways as we as we keep going and read the stories of uh of these sons."],[251.79,"And this has kind of shown us that, you know, as as we were reading Jacob's story, uh there's a there's a lot of uh con games, starting with his mother teaching him con games."],[266.95,"Then he goes to his uncle, his mother's brother."],[269.95,"He and his uncle Laban are conning and counter-conning each other and trying to work each other and get around each other the whole time of their relationship, which ended very badly."],[280.19,"Part of that is what created the polygamous situation he's in"],[287.43,"And so we see not only like the consequences of polygamy, not only the consequences of someone showing favoritism within his own family, but we're seeing the consequences of those are really included in the consequences of how Jacob has lived his life."],[309.85,"How Israel lived his life."],[311.69,"Remember, Israel, the name it was changed to, uh, means struggles with God."],[318.57,"Not submits to God, not obeys God, not as faithful to God, but wrestles with God."],[323.79,"And so uh these consequences that are playing out in his family are consequences of that."],[332.71,"And we've seen that as sort of an overarching theme as we've been going through the patriarchal narratives about how the character of a father sort of plays out in the life of his son."],[345.0,"And some of these sort of generational things, none of which are sort of curses like you're doomed to, you know, repeat."],[354.35,"But that these things have consequences."],[357.47,"And this is going to continue to be a feature of, this is just a feature of the narrative portions of the Hebrew Bible, which is one of the many things that makes them very different than the kind of stories we get from other ancient Near Eastern sources, which are trying to do the opposite of humanize their subjects."],[377.85,"So if you have a historical founder figure like Abraham in an ancient Near Eastern text, it's going to be an epic."],[387.13,"Abraham is not going to like get scared and lie."],[390.65,"Abraham is not going to have any marital problems."],[396.09,"Abraham is not going to none of these things are going to happen."],[398.65,"They're going to be presented as this hero, this sort of perfect, admirable character."],[404.63,"And the reason I'm kind of belaboring this a little bit is because it really puts to lie the idea that some people have, which is not an orthodox idea, that the sort of quote unquote literal, and that's a whole can of worms, but that the base meaning."],[433.21,"The basic meaning of the narratives in scripture should just be tossed aside in favor of some kind of allegory."],[441.82,"Because that's not the kind of storytelling and writing that this is."],[447.38,"When you're writing something that you intend to be interpreted that way."],[452.03,"You don't go into the details about relationships, you don't go into character flaws, you don't have characters who have an arc where they learn and grow and change over time."],[462.41,"If you're attending for it to be interpreted solely in an allegorical way, then you have figures who just represent principles or ideas."],[474.86,"When you're attending that kind of interpretation."],[477.42,"Now, that isn't to say that there aren't levels of interpretation above and beyond, in addition to that basic meaning."],[488.0,"I'm not saying basic meaning and then stop."],[490.9,"There's obviously more depths of meaning, to the Bible than that, as you dig deeper into it."],[496.75,"But you get to those through the basic meaning."],[502.47,"Through stories of actual people who actually lived and actually existed and were a lot like us in good and bad ways."],[512.84,"It's through that that we get to those deeper levels, not by sort of setting that aside or pretending it's not there."],[520.44,"And this has kind of been a theme in the Bible study."],[523.02,"Remember, for example, when we started Romans, first of St."],[527.42,"Paul's epistles we wrote, and I pointed out this is a letter."],[530.54,"This is not a theological treatise."],[532.86,"Philosophical and theological treatises existed in the ancient world."],[536.3,"St. Paul could have written one if he wanted to."],[539.42,"This is a letter he wrote."],[540.54,"Same kind of thing here"],[544.34,"We could have gotten an epic of Abraham."],[549.63,"That kind of thing existed."],[551.79,"At the time this was put together."],[554.27,"That's not what we got."],[557.45,"And that's significant."],[559.87,"What the the shape of what we have is significant."],[564.59,"And if we saw bits and pieces of that with Abraham and Isaac and even Jacob as we go forward through the rest of Genesis, it's going to get very earthy, shall we say?"],[579.53,"Even more so."],[581.26,"Um as we continue and and finish up."],[586.19,"This this isn't the genre of mythology."],[590.93,"This is not the genre of epic."],[595.01,"This is not the genre of folktale."],[597.95,"This is the genre of the memory of previous generations of your people and and what they experienced and what they what they went through."],[613.87,"So, all that said, by way of recap and my general rambling, of which I am wont."],[621.19,"We'll go ahead, unless there are any leftover comments or questions or comments on how painful it is to read David Hume."],[631.35,"We'll pick up in Genesis chapter 35, verse 1."],[639.11,"And God said to Jacob, Arise, go up to Bethel and dwell there, and make an altar there to God, who appeared to you when you fled from the face of Esau, your brother."],[658.55,"Town just got wiped out."],[661.43,"Might not be good to hang around these parts."],[664.55,"And God tells Jacob to go back to Bethel"],[668.0,"Remember, Bethel's the place where he stopped, where he saw the ziggurat."],[671.92,"Jacob's ladder that we talked about."],[674.64,"And God even reiterates."],[676.18,"But notice how it's summarized."],[680.34,"It's summarized as God appearing to him."],[683.14,"It's not where you saw the ladder or where you saw the ziggurat or where you saw the angels."],[687.62,"It's where God appeared to you."],[689.15,"That's the summary."],[691.15,"Um. It's also interesting that God is speaking and he says, make an altar there to God who appeared to you."],[705.23,"But I'll just leave that there for now, because I don't want to go on another ramble about the first and second persons of the Trinity."],[710.83,"We've done enough of that in Genesis."],[712.59,"See previous Bible studies."],[716.47,"Verse 2 that Jacob said to his household and to all who are with him, put away the foreign gods among you, purify yourselves, and change your garments."],[726.23,"Let us arise and go up to Bethel, and make there an altar to God who answered me in the day of my tribulation, and was with me and saved me on the road I traveled."],[738.15,"So they gave Jacob all the foreign gods in their hands and the earrings in their ears, and Jacob hid them under the terebinth tree near Shechem."],[747.95,"Um, so this is interesting."],[754.07,"This whole crew apparently has all these idols"],[761.4,"This is of course the wives, the servants slash concubines, the kids, but also all the other servants."],[771.05,"This huge multitude he's traveling with."],[773.45,"It's apparently mostly pagan."],[776.67,"Mostly pagan. Would they be worshiping Yahweh as one of their gods?"],[782.67,"No, because this says to your foreign gods."],[786.11,"Idols to your foreign gods."],[789.55,"So that's explicitly saying other gods."],[794.99,"Um but that frames what's going on here."],[802.47,"This isn't just hey, uh, I hear you need to pull up stakes, Jacob."],[809.51,"Here's a place you can head."],[811.8,"What's the danger."],[813.09,"Jacob is worried."],[816.61,"He and his whole family are going to be slaughtered when someone comes to take revenge for what his sons did."],[823.11,"He's frightened. When has he been in that situation before?"],[829.27,"When he thought Esau was gonna kill him."],[831.64,"And so God comes to him and says, You remember that."],[835.96,"You remember when you were scared your brother Esau was going to kill you and you fled."],[840.6,"And I appeared to you at this place."],[844.12,"That God, this God, right"],[849.52,"Yahweh, appeared to him at that place."],[854.27,"This is a call, for Jacob to repent and return to him."],[861.4,"To say, oh, yeah."],[864.12,"God protected me then, God will protect me now, but what?"],[868.92,"But I've got to make things right."],[873.1,"Which he knows aren't right."],[875.66,"That's right. Because he knows there's this idolatry going on."],[880.68,"He doesn't have a long list of commandments yet, but he can figure that much out."],[884.86,"If you're gonna r relyely on on the true God, you can't also be kind ofing on a whole bunch of otheres."],[894.39,"And then he tells them to, as a sign of repentance, to wash themselves and wash their clothing."],[906.53,"And he says, you know what?"],[909.73,"God's right. In the day when I was in trouble."],[913.13,"He answered me and he appeared to me."],[915.23,"And so we're gonna go and we're gonna build an altar and we're gonna offer sacrifices."],[921.23,"Is a terebinth tree significant in anyway?"],[925.55,"Well, so it seems like this is referring to a tree that at least somebody among the early readers would have like an actual location."],[936.8,"Like when we talked about in it was the oak of memory."],[940.98,"That God appears up under that oak."],[944.56,"Like this is a place at least the original readers would have known where that was."],[950.9,"So when it says the terebinth tree that's near Shechem."],[955.06,"That's why the landmark didn't."],[956.74,"Yeah, yeah. It's like, you know."],[958.9,"Yeah, there are trees that are landmarks like that today."],[961.38,"Yeah, yeah, because trees live a long time."],[964.82,"Trees can live a very long time."],[967.38,"Yeah. So in the in the Hebrew, it actually says where they remain until this day."],[970.9,"til this day Un very."],[975.18,"So you can figure out when that is."],[978.0,"Um but the reason I know that is that it's one of the verses Saint Jerome points to."],[983.36,"When somebody said to it pointed the verse in Matthew to him that says that the Theotokos or that that St."],[990.48,"Joseph did not know the Theotokos until she had given birth to Jesus."],[993.92,"And he was like, see, it says until so that means that they did afterwards."],[998.8,"And he's like, Really?"],[1000.08,"So like when this verse says they remained there until this very day, that means today we found them."],[1009.05,"It's like, oh no, it doesn't mean that."],[1011.45,"It means we still haven't found them."],[1013.31,"Until does not necessarily. mean that there's a change at that point"],[1025.52,"Verse 5. Then Israel departed from Shechem."],[1029.84,"This is Israel, the person."],[1032.45,"Israel departed from Shechem, and the fear of God was upon all the cities around them."],[1038.05,"Thus they did not pursue the sons of Jacob."],[1042.69,"So the idea here is that because Jacob is faithful."],[1050.67,"God is protecting him and his family."],[1054.0,"That that's the reason."],[1055.52,"They didn't fear Jacob."],[1056.72,"There wasn't something, it was God"],[1063.0,"Now Jacob came to Luz, that is Bethel, in the land of Canaan."],[1068.12,"He and all the people with him."],[1070.2,"Luz is what it was called before it was called Bethel."],[1075.4,"He built an altar there and called the place Bethel because their God appeared to him when he fled from the face of his brother Esau."],[1085.48,"Now Deborah, Rebecca's nurse died."],[1088.44,"What does Bethel mean?"],[1090.52,"Well, when he named it, this is the second time he's referred to it as Bethel."],[1094.04,"So he's not naming it now, but we talked about last time."],[1096.64,"It's Baat El means the the house or the temple of God."],[1101.8,"Okay. Yeah Yeah."],[1103.52,"Bait El. Now Deborah, Rebecca's nurse, died, and she was buried below Bethel under the oak."],[1114.16,"So Jacob called its name the Oak of Mourning."],[1116.4,"So again, you have this, you know, the Oak."],[1120.48,"There by Bethel."],[1121.76,"What do they call the Oak of Mourning?"],[1129.04,"Now, by the way, we haven't seen anything about Deborah or Rebecca's nurse before this."],[1134.78,"So, why is this here?"],[1137.1,"It's telling us the origin of the name of the tree."],[1142.6,"So, once again, we've got this series of landmarks. these actual places"],[1148.32,"Which again reinforces what I was saying before about the fact that this is stuff happening in the real world."],[1153.7,"Where you could go and see where it was."],[1155.78,"But also, this is because these are places where people made pilgrimages."],[1163.54,"There are places where people went to see where these great saints, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, had these things happen, these holy places."],[1177.82,"That's the old testament."],[1180.48,"They were making these pilgrimages."],[1182.36,"So this idea making pilgrimages to burial sites, making pilgrimages, that's why it's here."],[1188.84,"In the Torah in the Pentateuch, why we're given these geographical locators"],[1198.96,"Verse 9. Then God appeared to Jacob again when he came from Mesopotamia and blessed him."],[1205.12,"God said to him, Your name is Jacob."],[1207.52,"Your name shall not anymore be called Jacob, but Israel shall be your name."],[1212.0,"God also said to him, I am your God, increase and multiply."],[1215.92,"A nation and a company of nations shall proceed from you, and kings shall come from your body."],[1221.84,"The land I gave Abraham and Isaac I give to you, and to your seed after you I give this land."],[1228.32,"Then God ascended from him from him in the place where he talked with him."],[1233.36,"So Jacob set up a pillar in the. place where he talked with him, a pillar of stone, and he poured a drink offering on it, and he poured oil on it"],[1241.4,"Then Jacob called the name of the place where God spoke with him, Bethel."],[1244.93,"So again, when it says he called it Bethel, this is not, he named it that three times."],[1253.38,"It's talking about the history of the place."],[1257.56,"Bethel. This is the place where God appeared to him not once, but multiple times."],[1263.23,"But notice what happens here at the end is God appears to him again and reiterates the promises that were made to Abraham that that had been had hand beened. handed down to Isaac down to him"],[1282.47,"So here we're at the culmination of the story of Jacob."],[1289.55,"Because what has happened?"],[1291.07,"Well, first he reconciled with Esau."],[1293.31,"He reconciled with his brother."],[1297.52,"And now what's happened?"],[1300.56,"Now he's reconciled himself with God."],[1309.76,"So at the beginning of his story, his chicanery got him into trouble, and he ran away."],[1317.25,"And he went on this jour joneyur andney, and God was with him on the, God blessed him on that journey."],[1322.17,"But he was still kind of living by his wits."],[1324.51,"And we've seen all the things that happened."],[1328.64,"And now we've got the mature."],[1331.89,"The Jacob at the end of his story."],[1334.05,"And again, there's trouble."],[1337.41,"Now it's caused by his sons, not by him."],[1341.3,"And what does he do?"],[1343.14,"This time, he shows faithfulness to God."],[1347.62,"He follows God."],[1349.06,"He offers the sacrifices and is reconciled to God, is the last step of the process."],[1354.1,"And he has those promises of God that he's inherited reiterated to him."],[1360.18,"So this is sort of showing the end of his ark."],[1361.3,"The way Abraham's ark sort of ended with the sacrifice of Isaac"],[1367.64,"Is this display of his trust at the end of his arc of learning to trust God and put his trust in the promises of God?"],[1374.36,"This is Jacob now learning, I need to not live by my wits."],[1391.28,"Notice Jacob is reconciled with his fellow human beings."],[1397.86,"And that's sort of a precondition for him being reconciled to God."],[1405.24,"This idea of forgiving others, and through forgiving others, receiving the forgiveness of God is not something that Christ just makes up in the Gospels."],[1416.13,"This is built into the pattern that we see, that we see in Scripture."],[1425.73,"But also, Jacob, and it's reiterated here, is named Israel."],[1432.53,"He's the guy who the Israelites we're going to be reading about the Old Testament are descended from."],[1440.25,"And one of the big thematic elements of the whole rest of the Old Testament is going to be the fact that Israel again and again and again, the people of God again and again and again, are going to try and live by their own wits rather than trusting in God."],[1462.81,"They're not going to trust God to protect them."],[1464.81,"So they're going to make military alliances."],[1467.13,"They're going to end up being disasters."],[1488.9,"Over and over and over and over again."],[1492.75,"So the lesson that Israel, the person, has learned here in his life is a lesson to be communicated to Israel, the people of God."],[1505.97,"Israel, the people of God."],[1509.57,"That God is not the person you run to after you've messed everything up and tried everything you can think of to get out of it, and nothing has worked, and now this is your last resort."],[1522.07,"God should be our first resort."],[1524.47,"We should be trusting in him all along."],[1526.79,"I mean, if we're really doing that, we probably will get ourselves into a lot less trouble in the first place."],[1534.43,"But we will definitely not bring disaster upon ourselves through our further dealings."],[1542.97,"Because none of Jacob's cons really worked out that well for him."],[1550.22,"He's got two wives who have a bitter rivalry with each other."],[1554.7,"The sons of the wife he doesn't love are kind of out of control because they're aware that he doesn't love their mother."],[1561.74,"And by out of control, I mean murdered a whole town."],[1565.71,"Like potentially bringing disaster on the whole family."],[1569.76,"He hasn't really gotten away with anything."],[1575.69,"But here at the end we see. how he's progressed and that he's learned this lesson that now hopefully his descendants will also learn"],[1583.33,"But we know most of them won't."],[1586.37,"Spoilers. Interesting because you said uh previously that it's like a spiral that you kind of repeat the same thing in um Rachel had uh idols underneath her earlier, and then but she didn't really get rid of them."],[1603.65,"She kept it kind of like uh it's almost the same pattern and now they get rid of 'em."],[1611.49,"Now they get rid of 'em."],[1612.45,"Yeah. Um. I had encountered the interpretation that um since Jacob is Israel, founder of Israel, that these stories were supposed to show how clever and devious he was and go and do likewise."],[1640.81,"Yeah, no. Yeah."],[1644.81,"So that's yeah."],[1648.17,"That is an extraordinarily bad way to read narrative."],[1651.93,"But it's an unfortunately very common way to read narrative."],[1656.11,"So everyone assumes, like countless people, even fairly intelligent people will look at you and say, well, uh, polygamy was okay in the Old Testament."],[1670.85,"Because, see, look, Abraham's polygamous, Jacob's polygamous."],[1677.41,"And it's like, have you have you read the story?"],[1679.89,"Have you read what happens?"],[1681.57,"Or there's child sacrifice in the Bible."],[1684.13,"Yeah, yeah. So the fact that something is narrated doesn't mean it's being endorsed."],[1692.47,"Especially when you show the consequences."],[1697.61,"Negative consequences related to those actions."],[1701.81,"Um, and I don't know where that weird hermeneutical assumption comes from."],[1708.53,"But right Other than, as you're saying earlier, people haven't had to take a logic class."],[1715.71,"When when someone does something and it gets them in trouble and they do it again and it gets them in worse trouble and they do it again and and it wrecks their whole family."],[1723.79,"I don't see how you're saying go how how the end result of reading that would be, oh, this is what I should do."],[1731.17,"The argument was that the these origin stories of a people were always supposed to describe what the people were supposed to be like."],[1742.21,"So since he's a con man, they should be a man."],[1745.55,"That's the idea."],[1746.79,"Yeah. But the problem then is okay, where Abraham and Isaac con men."],[1755.31,"No. Yeah. Or are any of the are any of why are any of the sons of Israel?"],[1764.77,"The sons of Jacob, like the literal sons, are any of them really con men?"],[1770.93,"Who are the progenitors of the tribes?"],[1772.54,"See, that doesn't work."],[1775.12,"That's that now that you've given me more detail, that sounds more like the the sort of uh dollar store young that you get from Joseph Campbell."],[1787.68,"Where you say, okay, well, I have this ancient Near Eastern epic founder epic and this Greek. founder epic and this from the Hopi Indians and this from China"],[1801.96,"And in very broad strokes, I can say this about it."],[1808.74,"The heroes of these stories exhibit some characteristic that characterizes the culture that looks up to them."],[1815.46,"And then I'm gonna go and any story I read that I can fit into that mold, I'm gonna insist is doing the same thing."],[1823.3,"You can see the flaw there."],[1824.98,"Yeah. Um, that's what gives you the arguments about like, oh, all these people had virgin births in Greek and Roman mythology."],[1833.35,"No one had a virgin birth in Greek and Roman mythology."],[1837.19,"Anyone who says that to any atheist who tries to use that argument to you, I don't care who they are, I don't care if they have a PhD in something, they don't know what they're talking about."],[1847.31,"There are zero cases."],[1850.45,"The only way you get that is if you do the Joseph Campbell thing."],[1856.29,"I'm gonna say any non traditional birth is a virgin birth."],[1862.1,"I'm gonna say uh, you know, Zeus becoming a goose and impregnating a woman is a virgin birth."],[1871.46,"So I know I see some differences between that and the Gospel of St."],[1874.82,"Luke. I don't know."],[1875.94,"Like, I I feel like I've read both and they're not alike."],[1882.1,"So yeah, so."],[1883.02,"So that's why I would say, where are you getting that from the patriarchal narratives?"],[1890.54,"I mean, we've already pointed out tonight several ways in which the patriarchal narratives are different from those other stories."],[1898.51,"So yeah, those other stories, for example, as we said, wouldn't point out to anything that was considered a foible in the subject."],[1907.63,"But that doesn't mean you come to the patriarchal narratives and say, well, since these are a similar genre, these things must not be foibles."],[1915.87,"That doesn't that doesn't follow"],[1924.49,"Yeah, yeah. I mean, that."],[1926.73,"But see, to me, it's it's going in the opposite direction"],[1927.17,"Yeah. It's not a later culture that values those things projecting it onto their founder."],[1941.19,"It's a story about the founder that's intended to communicate certain things to the later culture and society with an ought attached to it."],[1954.6,"So that that's a different."],[1956.85,"It's not retroactively casting these things onto an unknown or fictional character."],[1962.69,"But a this is, you know, the person who we're descended from."],[1967.65,"Look at what he learned."],[1969.03,"Look at. the journey he took"],[1971.07,"We need to learn from that ourselves."],[1974.08,"Well, and that also makes sense in messianic hypotheses and everything."],[1978.0,"Right forward. So there may also be a bias in there that they think this is just made up by about a fictional character by some later."],[1988.99,"So there's so there are no facts underlying this, for example."],[1992.76,"Yeah. Okay. So we're 16."],[2001.56,"Then Jacob departed from Bethel, and when there was but a little distance to go to Ephraith, Rachel labored with much difficulty in childbirth."],[2013.52,"Now it came to pass when she was in hard labor that the midwife said to her, Take courage, you will have this son also."],[2021.36,"So it was, as her soul was departing, for she died."],[2025.04,"She called his name Ben Oni, but his father called him Benjamin."],[2031.52,"Thus Rachel died, and was buried on the way to Ephraith, that is Bethlehem."],[2039.12,"Then Jacob set a pillar on her grave, which is the pillar of Rachel's grave to this day."],[2044.0,"So again, we've got a marker"],[2045.9,"We've got a shrine, we've got a place to go on pilgrimage."],[2048.86,"So Rachel dies giving birth to her second son, uh, Benjamin."],[2055.02,"Uh Benoni means my son"],[2058.26,"Umin means son of my right hand."],[2062.82,"Uh which ends up being ironic because the tribe of Benjamin is going to be mostly left-handed."],[2068.5,"But that's going to come up later in the Old Testament."],[2072.42,"They're going to have a high incidence of left-handedness for whatever reason."],[2076.9,"Um, so here's another place where we get."],[2082.42,"Remember, I mentioned when we were talking about mosaic authorship, how there are these places where there are names dropped in from other time periods."],[2090.12,"So Ephraith is what this place was called at the time."],[2094.68,"Later on, it gets named Bethlehem, which is Beit Lechem, which is the house of bread."],[2100.76,"So you can guess what they grew admit there"],[2107.92,"But is there any significance to the coincidence that this is Bethlehem and Christ was born in Bethlehem?"],[2118.16,"Well, this is where David's gonna be born."],[2121.36,"So when we get to Ruth, uh we'll talk more about that."],[2126.93,"Because the book of Ruth unfolds around Bethlehem."],[2132.45,"Um the name of it becomes important."],[2135.57,"So I'm putting a pin in that until we get to the book of Ruth."],[2140.81,"But I will point out, particularly because again, we're told about this pillar."],[2150.73,"There's this shrine here of the grave of Rachel."],[2155.34,"Remember the verse?"],[2157.74,"The prophet, as Israel's going into exile, heard a voice in Rama, Rachel weeping for her children."],[2169.14,"What does that presuppose?"],[2174.26,"That Rachel still exists."],[2176.34,"That Rachel still exists and knows what's going on in the world."],[2181.7,"And who is she weeping to?"],[2185.7,"God. She's interceding with God."],[2190.47,"As they left this place."],[2193.35,"And where does that verse get quoted in the New Testament?"],[2198.87,"St. Matthew's gospel."],[2200.71,"When Herod goes and slaughters the innocents."],[2203.35,"All the babies who were born in Bethlehem."],[2210.65,"In Bethlehem. So we not only have pilgrimages to shrines here, this is the intercession of a saint at the shrine."],[2221.69,"There's not a way around this."],[2225.21,"I have pointed this out to Jewish people."],[2228.49,"And they had to yield the point."],[2232.37,"That's clearly what this is talking about in the Torah."],[2235.09,"And that's what Jewish people believed at the time."],[2239.99,"Whether they believe it now or not."],[2242.17,"That's it's not arguable that that's how this text was understood by pre Christian Jewish people and how it was used."],[2254.17,"So sorry to some of our Protestant friends who don't like that idea."],[2260.41,"But it's there."],[2262.65,"So it's in the Bible."],[2266.65,"I mean, what are you gonna do?"],[2268.33,"Verse 21. Now Israel journeyed and pitched his tent beyond the Tower of Eder."],[2274.49,"So it happened when Israel dwelt in that land that Reuben went and lay with Bilhah, his father's concubine."],[2282.37,"Israel heard about it and it was seen as evil in his sight."],[2285.73,"There's an understatement."],[2290.45,"Yeah. Yeah. So this is another one of those instances like we talked about, and since we talked about it with Ham and Canaan."],[2300.69,"I won't go into excruciating detail here, especially since it's only one verse."],[2305.33,"But remember, this is a way of attempting to uh take control of your father's house by sleeping with one of your father's wives, trying to produce an heir that way."],[2321.33,"es. Y Why would Reuben do this?"],[2326.41,"Ruben's in disgrace."],[2328.89,"Reuben got cut off because of what happened in Shechem."],[2333.05,"He was the firstborn son."],[2335.37,"Now he's on the outs."],[2338.41,"So he does this perverse and grotesque thing as a way of trying to seize the inheritance back."],[2349.38,"To seize the inheritance back."],[2352.5,"Do you see how this happening is kind of a dark shadow based on what's been going on in the family with Jacob and how he's lived his life and how that's playing out right in the next generation?"],[2370.67,"Jacob should not have been sleeping with Bill Hawt in the first place."],[2375.84,"On top of everything else."],[2377.81,"And so there are these consequences."],[2381.27,"There are these consequences."],[2382.95,"And we also see that uh Ruben, at least, has not learned the lesson that Jacob learned."],[2393.31,"Because Ruben's desperate to get his inheritance back."],[2400.35,"And thinks this way is going to work."],[2403.65,"How will it work?"],[2405.41,"Well, societally, if he produced a son with his father's wife, that would shame his father publicly, and Reuben's son would effectively become the heir of the household."],[2423.05,"Um, but this also shows something interesting in terms of how Reuben views his birthright."],[2431.77,"Because remember, the whole thing with Jacob and Esau was about the birthright."],[2435.13,"But the whole thing about the birthright was about the promises God made to Abraham."],[2439.77,"Pretty clearly, that's not what Reuben is thinking about."],[2442.41,"Because God isn't gonna honor this."],[2445.79,"Like, God isn't gonna be like, well, sorry, Jacob, he slept with your wife, so you know."],[2453.11,"Now I gotta give him the promised land."],[2455.53,"No, that's not happening very clearly."],[2458.54,"So he's after the material possessions."],[2462.94,"That's what he's concerned about."],[2466.07,"That's what he's trying to seize back."],[2468.81,"Not the spiritual blessings, not any of that."],[2476.17,"Yeah. And so, since Israel finds out about it, of course, it doesn't work."],[2484.03,"And this sort of fully ensures that Reuben is not going to be considered the firstborn."],[2490.75,"Now, we probably could have guessed that anyway."],[2494.47,"Who do you think Jacob is going to consider his firstborn?"],[2500.63,"Rachel's firstborn, which is Joseph."],[2503.03,"Yeah. You probably could have guessed that anyway."],[2507.37,"But see, this is this is an area of tension."],[2512.81,"And we're going to see this as the story of Joseph begins, is that frankly, Jacob is going to treat Joseph as his firstborn."],[2520.09,"But sort of legally, by the legal custom of the time, he isn't."],[2525.53,"Even if Reuben and Simeon get disinherited because of what they've done."],[2529.53,"Judah would be next."],[2531.13,"Well, Levi and then Judah."],[2533.05,"Yeah. Those two are next up."],[2536.84,"You gotta disinherit like 10 other people before you get to before you get to Joseph."],[2542.54,"Um there's no basis."],[2545.58,"Jacob has no basis for doing that."],[2547.9,"So that's going to cause this tension in the family."],[2551.52,"That we're going to see play out."],[2553.44,"Probably not tonight, but uh maybe next time."],[2562.4,"Verse 23. Now the sons of Jacob were 12."],[2565.68,"We get a recap here because we're setting up for the transition to talking about them."],[2571.68,"Sons of Jacob were 12."],[2573.28,"The sons of Leah were Reuben, Jacob's firstborn, and Simeon, Levi, Judah, Issachar, and Zebulun."],[2579.04,"The sons of Rachel were Joseph and Benjamin"],[2584.12,"The sons of Bilhah, Rachel's maidservant, were Dan and Naphtali."],[2588.52,"And the sons of Zilpah, Leah's maidservant, were Gad and Asher."],[2593.64,"These were the sons of Jacob who were born to him in Mesopotamia of Syria."],[2603.8,"Verse 27, that Jacob came to his father Isaac at Mamre, or Kiryath Arva, that is Hebron."],[2611.02,"So we actually get three names."],[2613.07,"So we've been calling it Mamre from Abraham on."],[2616.65,"It's also called Kiriath Arbah."],[2618.57,"And even more recently, it's called Hebron."],[2620.73,"So there's been these sort of updates, tacked in here for future readers."],[2622.97,"Where Abraham and Isaac sojourned"],[2629.41,"Now the days of Isaac were 180 years."],[2633.41,"So Isaac breathed his last and died, and was gathered to his people, being old and full of days."],[2640.29,"Thus his sons Esau and Jacob buried him."],[2644.05,"Remember, Abraham had been buried by Isaac and Ishmael at the time that he died."],[2651.33,"I'd forgotten that Isaac was still alive."],[2653.97,"Yeah. But very old."],[2658.93,"Yeah Now, by the way, it's 120 in the Hebrew."],[2663.41,"Uh herday. Remember what I've said about numbers?"],[2666.77,"Getting wonky. because we don't have numerals"],[2673.69,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2681.05,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2686.41,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.68,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 35: wrap-up of the Jacob stories","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Genesis 35</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Wrapping up stories of <b>Jacob and Esau</b>; getting ready for <b>Joseph</b></li><li class=\"d1\">doubt we get there tonight</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Wrap-up stage\" = heading toward <b>genealogies</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>death of previous generation</b> to set up the next</li><li class=\"d0\">Transition already started</li></ul>"},{"t":85.6,"lv":3,"h":"Recap: fallout of the Shechem massacre","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Last story: eldest sons (<b>Reuben</b>, <b>Simeon</b>) took brutal revenge on a whole town</li><li class=\"d1\">Jacob angry - position it put them in as <b>nomads</b></li><li class=\"d1\">had good relations with locals - \"things get awkward at the local gatherings\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jacob will have to <b>move, reorient</b>; also quite elderly</li><li class=\"d0\">Focus since the reconciliation: <b>bad family dynamics</b> in polygamous household</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Leah</b> - unloved, many kids (own + servant)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Rachel</b> - loved, one child so far + a few through servant</li><li class=\"d1\">favoritism \"not even thinly veiled\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Part of what caused the <b>massacre</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sons don't trust father to make things right → take matters into own hands</li></ul>"},{"t":251.79,"lv":3,"h":"Jacob's cons and the meaning of \"Israel\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jacob's story: <b>con games</b>, starting with mother teaching him</li><li class=\"d1\">uncle <b>Laban</b>: conning and counter-conning, ended very badly</li><li class=\"d1\">part of what created the polygamous situation</li><li class=\"d0\">Consequences of polygamy + favoritism = consequences of <b>how Jacob lived his life</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Israel</b> = \"struggles with God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not submits, obeys, is faithful - <b>wrestles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Overarching theme in patriarchal narratives: <b>father's character plays out in son's life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not curses you're doomed to repeat - but <b>things have consequences</b></li></ul>"},{"t":357.47,"lv":2,"h":"Biblical narrative vs ancient Near Eastern epic","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Feature of Hebrew Bible narrative: <b>humanizes</b> its subjects</li><li class=\"d1\">ANE sources do the opposite</li><li class=\"d0\">ANE founder like Abraham = an <b>epic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wouldn't get scared and lie; no marital problems</li><li class=\"d1\">\"perfect, admirable character,\" a hero</li><li class=\"d0\">Why belabor: puts to lie an idea that is <b>not Orthodox</b></li><li class=\"d1\">that the <b>basic meaning</b> of the narratives should be tossed for <b>allegory</b></li></ul>"},{"t":404.63,"lv":3,"h":"Against dropping the basic meaning","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not the kind of writing this is</li><li class=\"d1\">intended-allegory = no relationships, no <b>character flaws</b>, no arcs of learning and growth</li><li class=\"d1\">figures just represent principles</li><li class=\"d0\">Not saying basic meaning and stop</li><li class=\"d1\">more <b>depths of meaning</b> as you dig - \"but you get to those <b>through</b> the basic meaning\"</li><li class=\"d1\">through stories of <b>actual people</b> who lived and were like us, good and bad</li><li class=\"d0\">Same theme as <b>Romans</b>: \"this is a <b>letter</b>, not a theological treatise\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul could have written a treatise; he didn't</li><li class=\"d1\">we could have had an <b>epic of Abraham</b> - didn't get one → shape is significant</li></ul>"},{"t":564.59,"lv":3,"h":"The genre of Genesis","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Rest of Genesis gets \"very earthy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>mythology, epic, folktale</b></li><li class=\"d0\">It is the <b>memory of previous generations</b> of your people and what they went through</li><li class=\"d0\">Comments? (or on \"how painful it is to read <b>David Hume</b>\")</li></ul>"},{"t":631.35,"lv":2,"h":"35:1-4 - Go up to Bethel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God: \"Arise, go up to <b>Bethel</b>, dwell there, make an <b>altar</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"town just got wiped out - might not be good to hang around\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Bethel = ladder / <b>ziggurat</b> place - but summarized as \"where <b>God appeared</b> to you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God speaks: \"make an altar to God who appeared to you\" - first/second persons of Trinity, see previous studies</li><li class=\"d0\">Jacob to household: <b>put away foreign gods</b>, purify, change garments</li><li class=\"d1\">altar to God who answered \"in the day of my <b>tribulation</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they hand over idols + <b>earrings</b>; Jacob hides them under the <b>terebinth</b> near Shechem</li></ul>"},{"t":747.95,"lv":3,"h":"The pagan multitude and the call to repent","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Whole crew has <b>idols</b>: wives, concubines, kids, all servants</li><li class=\"d1\">huge multitude \"apparently mostly <b>pagan</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>foreign gods</b>\" - explicitly other gods, not Yahweh as one of them</li><li class=\"d0\">Not just \"here's a place you can head\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jacob is <b>frightened</b> of revenge slaughter - same as when he feared <b>Esau</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God: remember when you fled and I appeared to you there</li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>call to repent and return</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God protected me then, will now - but must make things right</li><li class=\"d1\">no long list of commandments yet, but can figure out: can't rely on the true God and a bunch of others</li><li class=\"d0\">Wash selves and clothing = <b>sign of repentance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Plan: build altar, offer sacrifices</li></ul>"},{"t":921.23,"lv":3,"h":"Q: the terebinth; \"until this day\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Is the <b>terebinth</b> significant?</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>landmark</b> original readers would know - like the <b>oak of Mamre</b></li><li class=\"d1\">trees live a long time</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew: where they remain \"<b>until this day</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Jerome</b>: Matthew's \"Joseph did not know her <b>until</b>\" - \"see, so afterwards they did\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jerome: then \"until this day\" means we found them today? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"Until\" doesn't necessarily mean a change</b> at that point</li></ul>"},{"t":1025.52,"lv":2,"h":"35:5-8 - Fear of God; Luz; Deborah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Israel (the person) leaves Shechem</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fear of God</b> on surrounding cities → they don't pursue</li><li class=\"d1\">because Jacob is faithful, <b>God protects</b> - \"they didn't fear Jacob\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Comes to <b>Luz, that is Bethel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Luz = earlier name</li><li class=\"d0\">Builds altar; <b>Bethel</b> = <i>Beit El</i>, house/temple of God</li><li class=\"d1\">not naming it now - second time he's called it that</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deborah</b>, Rebecca's nurse, dies - buried below Bethel under the oak</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>Oak of Mourning</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1129.04,"lv":3,"h":"Why Deborah, and landmarks as pilgrimage sites","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Nothing about Deborah before - why here? <b>Origin of the tree's name</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Series of <b>landmarks</b>, actual places</li><li class=\"d1\">reinforces: this happened in the <b>real world</b>, you could go see</li><li class=\"d0\">Also because these were <b>pilgrimage</b> places</li><li class=\"d1\">where <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b> had things happen; burial sites</li><li class=\"d1\">that's why the geographical locators are in the Pentateuch</li><li class=\"d1\">pilgrimage in the OT</li></ul>"},{"t":1198.96,"lv":2,"h":"35:9-15 - God appears again at Bethel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God appears when he came from Mesopotamia, <b>blesses</b> him</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Your name shall not be Jacob but <b>Israel</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I am your God, increase and multiply\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>nation and company of nations</b>; <b>kings</b> from your body</li><li class=\"d1\">land given to Abraham and Isaac → to you and your seed</li><li class=\"d0\">God ascends; Jacob sets up a <b>stone pillar</b>, pours <b>drink offering and oil</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Called the name Bethel\" - not naming it a third time, telling the <b>history of the place</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God appeared here <b>multiple times</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reiterates promises made to Abraham, handed down to Isaac, to him</li></ul>"},{"t":1282.47,"lv":3,"h":"The culmination of Jacob's story","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First reconciled with <b>Esau</b>; now reconciled with <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Beginning: chicanery → ran away</li><li class=\"d1\">God blessed him on journey, but still \"living by his wits\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Mature Jacob: trouble again, caused by his <b>sons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">this time <b>faithful</b>: follows God, sacrifices, reconciled, promises reiterated</li><li class=\"d0\">End of his arc - like Abraham's arc ending with <b>sacrifice of Isaac</b></li><li class=\"d1\">learning to <b>trust God's promises</b>, not live by his wits</li><li class=\"d0\">Reconciled with fellow humans = <b>precondition</b> for reconciliation with God</li><li class=\"d1\">forgiving others → receiving God's forgiveness: not something Christ \"makes up\" - built into the pattern of Scripture</li></ul>"},{"t":1425.73,"lv":3,"h":"The lesson for Israel the people","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Israel, the person → Israelites descended from him</li><li class=\"d0\">Theme of the rest of the OT: people of God <b>again and again live by their own wits</b></li><li class=\"d1\">make <b>military alliances</b> → disasters</li><li class=\"d0\">Lesson Jacob learned is for the <b>people of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God is not the <b>last resort</b> after everything else failed</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God should be our <b>first resort</b>\" - far less trouble in the first place</li><li class=\"d0\">Jacob's cons didn't work out</li><li class=\"d1\">two rival wives; unloved wife's sons \"out of control\" - <b>murdered a whole town</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"hasn't really gotten away with anything\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hopefully descendants learn it - \"but we know most of them won't. Spoilers.\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>Rachel's idols</b> earlier, kept them - spiral/same pattern; now they get rid of them</li></ul>"},{"t":1612.45,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: narrated does not mean endorsed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: interpretation that Jacob = founder, stories show how <b>clever and devious</b> to \"go and do likewise\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Yeah, no\" - <b>extraordinarily bad way to read narrative</b>, but common</li><li class=\"d0\">Same logic: \"polygamy was okay in the OT - Abraham, Jacob\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"have you read what happens?\" Or child sacrifice in the Bible</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Narrated ≠ endorsed</b>, esp. when <b>negative consequences</b> shown</li><li class=\"d0\">Argument: origin stories describe what people should be like → they should be con men</li><li class=\"d1\">but are <b>Abraham, Isaac</b> con men? Are any of the <b>sons of Jacob</b>, progenitors of the tribes?</li></ul>"},{"t":1775.12,"lv":3,"h":"\"Dollar store Jung\" and Joseph Campbell","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sounds like <b>Joseph Campbell</b></li><li class=\"d1\">take founder epics (ANE, Greek, Hopi, China) → in broad strokes heroes show a trait of the culture</li><li class=\"d1\">then insist any story fitting the mold does the same</li><li class=\"d0\">Same flaw gives \"<b>virgin births</b> in Greek and Roman mythology\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>none</b> - \"zero cases\" - whatever the PhD</li><li class=\"d1\">only way: any non-traditional birth = virgin birth, e.g. <b>Zeus as a goose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">compare with <b>Luke</b> - \"they're not alike\"</li><li class=\"d0\">ANE epics wouldn't point out foibles - doesn't follow that Genesis's foibles aren't foibles</li></ul>"},{"t":1927.17,"lv":3,"h":"Direction of the story: founder to later culture","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not a later culture projecting values onto an unknown founder</li><li class=\"d0\">A story about the founder told to communicate to the later society \"with an <b>ought</b> attached\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the person we're descended from - look at what he learned, the journey he took; we need to learn from it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Audience: bias that it's made up by some later author, so <b>no facts underlying</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1992.76,"lv":2,"h":"35:16-20 - Rachel dies; Benjamin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Little distance from <b>Ephrath</b>, Rachel in hard labor</li><li class=\"d1\">midwife: \"Take courage, you will have this son also\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Dying, calls him <b>Ben-Oni</b> = my son (of sorrow); Jacob calls him <b>Benjamin</b> = son of my right hand</li><li class=\"d1\">ironic: tribe of Benjamin mostly <b>left-handed</b> - comes up later</li><li class=\"d0\">Buried on the way to Ephrath = <b>Bethlehem</b>; Jacob sets <b>pillar</b> on grave \"to this day\"</li><li class=\"d1\">another marker, a <b>shrine</b>, pilgrimage place</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ephrath</b> = name at the time; later <b>Bethlehem</b> = <i>Beit Lechem</i>, house of bread</li><li class=\"d1\">like names dropped in from other periods (Mosaic authorship)</li></ul>"},{"t":2107.92,"lv":3,"h":"Bethlehem and \"Rachel weeping\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Significance that Christ was born in <b>Bethlehem</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">where <b>David</b> is born; <b>Ruth</b> unfolds around Bethlehem - pin until then</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophet: \"voice in <b>Ramah</b>, Rachel weeping for her children\" as Israel goes into exile</li><li class=\"d1\">presupposes Rachel <b>still exists, knows what's going on</b></li><li class=\"d1\">weeping to <b>God</b> = <b>interceding</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Quoted in <b>Matthew</b>: Herod slaughters the innocents of Bethlehem</li><li class=\"d0\">So: pilgrimage to shrines + <b>intercession of a saint at the shrine</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not a way around this\"; Jewish people he's told had to yield the point</li><li class=\"d1\">how <b>pre-Christian Jews</b> understood it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"sorry to some Protestant friends... it's in the Bible\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2268.33,"lv":2,"h":"35:21-22 - Reuben and Bilhah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Israel pitches tent beyond the <b>Tower of Eder</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Reuben lay with Bilhah</b>, father's concubine; Israel hears - \"evil in his sight\" (understatement)</li><li class=\"d0\">Like Ham and Canaan: way to <b>take control of father's house</b>, produce an heir</li><li class=\"d0\">Why? Reuben firstborn but <b>in disgrace after Shechem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">perverse attempt to <b>seize the inheritance back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">would shame father publicly; his son would be <b>heir of the household</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Dark shadow of how Jacob lived</li><li class=\"d1\">Jacob shouldn't have been with <b>Bilhah</b> in the first place</li><li class=\"d1\">Reuben has <b>not learned Jacob's lesson</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Birthright was about God's <b>promises to Abraham</b> - Reuben thinking of <b>material possessions</b>, not spiritual blessings</li><li class=\"d1\">God won't give the promised land because he slept with Jacob's wife</li></ul>"},{"t":2494.47,"lv":3,"h":"Joseph as de facto firstborn","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reuben fully loses firstborn standing</li><li class=\"d0\">Jacob's firstborn in his mind: <b>Rachel's firstborn, Joseph</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Tension: Jacob treats Joseph as firstborn - legally he isn't</li><li class=\"d1\">even with Reuben and Simeon disinherited: next <b>Levi, then Judah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"disinherit like 10 other people before Joseph\" - no basis</li><li class=\"d1\">tension plays out in Joseph's story - maybe next time</li></ul>"},{"t":2562.4,"lv":2,"h":"35:23-29 - The twelve sons; Isaac's death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Recap setting up the transition: <b>12 sons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Leah: <b>Reuben</b>, Simeon, Levi, Judah, Issachar, Zebulun</li><li class=\"d1\">Rachel: <b>Joseph, Benjamin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Bilhah: <b>Dan, Naphtali</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Zilpah: <b>Gad, Asher</b></li><li class=\"d1\">born in Mesopotamia of Syria</li><li class=\"d0\">Jacob comes to <b>Isaac at Mamre</b> = <b>Kiriath Arba</b> = <b>Hebron</b></li><li class=\"d1\">three names - updates tacked in for later readers</li><li class=\"d0\">Isaac dies, \"old and full of days,\" gathered to his people</li><li class=\"d1\">buried by <b>Esau and Jacob</b> - as Abraham was by Isaac and Ishmael</li><li class=\"d1\">forgot Isaac was still alive - very old</li><li class=\"d1\">age given as 180; he says \"<b>120 in the Hebrew</b>\" - numbers get wonky without numerals</li></ul>"}]});
